\section*{Hellinger contraction with biased Boolean outputs}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 119; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that the Hellinger loss inequality holds for every Boolean output bias and all correlations, with signed coordinates attaining the bound and an information-theoretic consequence.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
Let $X$ be uniform on $\{-1,1\}^n$ and independently multiply each coordinate by a sign with mean $\rho\in[-1,1]$ to obtain $Y$. Set $T_{\rho} f(x)=\mathbb E[f(Y)\mid X=x]$, $H(t)=\sqrt{1-t^2}$ and $m=\mathbb Ef$. For every $n\ge1$ and Boolean $f:\{-1,1\}^n\to\{-1,1\}$, the claimed inequality is
\[
 H(m)-\mathbb E H(T_{\rho} f)\le1-H(\rho).
\]
Both expectations use uniform measure, and $m$ need not be zero.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 119; 2026-09-24), asymmetric dimension induction separates compressed small coordinates from the central band. A calibrated semigroup-energy estimate controls the remaining bias range, with exact Bernstein and interval certificates validating the scalar interface.

